**What appears to be happening:** Your potato plants are showing signs of early blight (Alternaria solani), a fungal disease that causes dark brown/black spots with concentric rings, especially on older lower leaves first. The yellowing and browning spreading throughout the foliage matches this condition well. This disease thrives in humid weather with alternating wet/dry cycles - exactly what you're experiencing right now.

**Current situation:** Unfortunately, there's currently NO safe spray window available for 30 days ahead in Nashik district due to rain (moderate chance of moderate rainfall on Aug 13, 20 and Sep 3) and high wind risk (~53%) that would cause drift issues if you tried spraying.

**What this means:** You should wait until after the next dry spell when weather conditions become favorable again before considering any treatment for early blight or late blight on your potatoes. The disease needs specific management - removing infected plant debris and applying fungicides at first appearance of symptoms, repeated every 15 days with different chemical modes to prevent resistance.

**Next steps:** Monitor the plants closely during this waiting period. If you notice new spots appearing after a dry spell comes up, then consult an expert or KVK about appropriate fungicide options for your specific situation and crop stage.
